Introduction
Sooner or later you end up with two forecasters, and the question that follows is rarely asked with enough rigor: is the new one actually better, or does it just look better on the chart you happened to be staring at? Equity curves answer this poorly - two curves can cross a dozen times and still leave you guessing whether the separation is real skill or favorable noise. What's needed is a formal test on the two series of forecast errors that accounts for serial correlation and small-sample behavior before calling a difference significant.
That test exists. Diebold and Mariano built it in 1995 for comparing macroeconomic forecasters, and it travels well to trading: given two loss-differential series, it returns a statistic and a p-value saying whether the difference in accuracy is distinguishable from zero. This article implements it natively in MQL5, with Newey-West HAC variance correction and the Harvey-Leybourne-Newbold small-sample adjustment - the latter starts to matter once you're down to a few hundred bars. An EA and a subwindow indicator both run it live and show a rolling verdict.
Scope note:
this article builds a comparison engine first and a trading layer second. The EA runs two forecasters side by side, computes a live rolling DM statistic, renders a color-coded verdict dashboard, and only then trades the currently-favored model's direction so the verdict carries a real trade count - the dashboard's answer is what's worth trusting; the trading logic exists to make that answer measurable, not to be a finished strategy.
Why equity curves lie to you
One terminology note: everywhere this article says "return," it means the raw bar-to-bar price change (close[t] - close[t-1]), not a percentage or log return - the DM math doesn't care which convention you use, as long as both sides of the comparison use the same one.
Comparing cumulative loss levels directly is weak: forecast errors in financial time series are heteroskedastic and autocorrelated - easy, low-volatility bars push both models' errors down together, a volatility spike does the opposite - so a naive t-test assuming independent observations will happily report significance on what's really just shared exposure to the same volatility regime. The DM test survives this by working with the loss differential series instead of loss levels, estimating its long-run variance with a heteroskedasticity-and-autocorrelation-consistent (HAC) estimator rather than the naive sample variance - the part most from-scratch implementations skip, and the part that actually makes the test trustworthy on financial data. Section 3 covers how that estimator behaves at this article's default one-step horizon.
The math: loss differentials, HAC variance, and the HLN correction
Let e1_t and e2_t be the forecast errors of Model A and Model B at bar t - realized price change minus predicted price change. Choose a loss function L(.), typically squared error for continuous forecasts, and build the loss differential d_t = L(e1_t) - L(e2_t) for every bar; a negative d_t means Model A had the lower loss. Everything downstream operates on this single series, packed into one small struct:
ENUM_DM_LOSS and DMResult
