The CDF comes from the regularized incomplete beta function, and the quantile from bisection on it, which was spot-checked against scipy.stats.t on a handful of df/p combinations during development and tracked closely. Fig. 2 shows how the Bartlett weights decay for different horizons. At h = 1 the sum collapses entirely - no lag terms survive, and the estimator reduces to plain sample variance. That's a deliberate edge case, not an oversight: one-step-ahead forecasts (the default in this article) don't have the overlapping-forecast autocorrelation that longer horizons introduce. What that protects against, at any horizon, is specifically the serial dependence overlapping multi-step forecasts induce - it isn't a general fix for every source of autocorrelation a loss differential could have, so volatility-clustering-driven dependence unrelated to forecast overlap can still sit uncorrected at h=1.
Fig. 1. Data flow from two live forecasters through the loss differential, HAC variance, and HLN-corrected DM statistic, into the rolling verdict dashboard.
Fig. 2. Bartlett kernel weights across lags for several forecast horizons h. At h=1 the sum has no lag terms; longer horizons let more distant autocovariances contribute, tapering linearly to zero.
The raw DM statistic, tested against a standard normal distribution, is only valid asymptotically, with hundreds or thousands of independent observations - a luxury MetaTrader backtests rarely give cleanly. Harvey, Leybourne, and Newbold's 1997 correction fixes this by rescaling the statistic and testing it against a Student-t distribution with n-1 degrees of freedom instead. Its practical effect scales with the horizon: at h=1 the h(h-1) term vanishes and the rescaling factor is close to 1 (roughly 0.998 at n=250), growing more consequential as h increases. Everything above comes together in one function that's the actual entry point the EA and the indicator both call:
