Three things worth calling out in that signature. The h(h-1) term inside the square root must be divided by n before being added to (n+1-2h) - get the order wrong and the bug is invisible at h=1 (where h(h-1)=0) but drifts from the real correction at longer horizons. correction_numerator isn't guaranteed positive: for a horizon large relative to n it can go negative, which would hand MathSqrt() a negative argument and silently return NaN into hln_stat, pvalue, and verdict - the explicit check above catches that and returns INVALID_HORIZON instead. And min_sample_size / significance_alpha are manifest-driven parameters, not hardcoded literals, which matters at the sample sizes a few months of M5 data with a short rolling window actually gives you: without the HLN correction you'll over-reject the null and convince yourself of edges that aren't there.
Choosing a loss function: squared vs absolute error
The DM test doesn't care which loss function you pick, as long as both sides use the same one - the math works identically for squared or absolute error, since the test only ever looks at the differential d_t (that's the branch inside DM_LossDifferential() above). The choice still matters practically: squared error punishes large misses disproportionately, which usually suits a position-sizing layer since an occasional wildly wrong prediction is more dangerous than a slightly higher average miss; absolute error treats every unit of error the same, which suits logic that only cares about directional correctness and shouldn't let a handful of volatility-spike bars dominate.
The manifest exposes this as a single loss_type field rather than a recompile-required constant, so you can rerun the same error logs through both and see if your conclusion survives the choice. If Model A wins under squared error but loses under absolute error, that's not a contradiction - it wins on average but loses on typical-case accuracy, usually by flagging rare large moves better and routine ones worse.
Two concrete forecasters
To make the test tangible rather than abstract, the EA runs two forecasters against each other continuously:
Model A - ONNX regression forecaster
. An 8-feature model predicting next-bar price change, exported through a standard sklearn-to-ONNX pipeline: three ATR-normalized lagged price changes, ATR-to-price ratio, scaled RSI, ATR-normalized MACD histogram, EMA slope, and log-scaled real volume.
Model B - EMA-slope baseline
. The slope of a 20-period EMA, used directly as the next-bar price-change prediction - no ML, the benchmark a more complex model has to actually beat.
Both models predict at bar close t the price change realized at t+1. The EA waits one bar, computes both realized errors, and appends them to two growing history arrays that the static and rolling DM tests both operate on. The EA and the indicator in Section 8 call the exact same two functions below rather than each keeping its own copy:
